      "id": 1,
      "text": "Mormon pioneers established Benson in the spring of 1871 while seeking additional farmland in the Cache Valley.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        8
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/benson_utah"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Benson was named in honor of Apostle Ezra T. Benson at the suggestion of Bishop Preston.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/benson_utah"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The Northwestern band of Shoshone people used the land around Benson for hunting, fishing, and gathering before Euro-American arrival.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        7
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/benson_utah"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The swift currents of the Bear River once required a horse-pulled ferry in Benson before a bridge was constructed.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        6
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/benson_utah"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Benson Elementary School is recognized on the National Register of Historic Places.",
